  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This file declares a static profile. Plain text has no grammar and never\n invokes the grammar-node attachment callback. LLVM emits no line counts\n for the initializer. The public `plain_line_fingerprints_use_sha256_words_without_losing_boundaries`\n test exercises this profile through `OutlineContent::from_source`, including\n its fingerprint scheme, whitespace normalization, and distinct word boundaries."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/plain.rs` meets its stated criteria.\n\n- **Static profile.** The file's only executable content is the `PROFILE` static, `static[PROFILE]`. It sets `language: None`, `kinds: &[]` and `attachment_target: |_| None`. That matches the commentary's description of a static profile with no grammar.\n- **The callback never runs.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/mod.rs#fn[parse_grammar]` sends a profile with no grammar to `plain_text`. Only the tree-walking `Builder` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/mod.rs#impl[Builder<'tree, 'source>]/fn[walk]` calls `attachment_target`, and plain text never reaches it. The closure body is therefore never executed, so LLVM has no line counts for it. This supports the claim that plain text never invokes the callback.\n- **The named test exists.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[plain_line_fingerprints_use_sha256_words_without_losing_boundaries]` goes through `OutlineContent::from_source`, per the commentary's description. It asserts a fixed SHA-256 digest for `hello`. It asserts that `two words` and ` two \\t words ` give equal fingerprints. It asserts that `twowords` differs from `two words`. It asserts the scheme string `plain-text-structural-v1:own-v1`. Those are the fingerprint scheme, whitespace normalization and word boundaries the commentary claims.\n- **The explanation is substantive.** It gives a specific reason the file's instrumented-line coverage is incomplete, and it does not rest on difficulty alone. It agrees with the current source and with the callers it relies on.\n- **No role problem.** The citation is a built-in coverage exception, and the file does not also mark `habeas:coverage` with {impl} or {test}, so there is no redundancy.",
